.TH GRANTCTL 8 "November 2026" "grantctl 1.0.1" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
grantctl \- manage accounts, SSH, sudo and resource limits across a small cluster from one file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B grantctl
.RI [ options ]
.I command
.RI [ arguments ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B grantctl
keeps the users of a small cluster in step with one file,
.IR /etc/grantctl/access.conf .
Each line names a user and the
.I roles
and
.I flags
the user holds, optionally limited to some nodes.
.PP
Run grantctl on the
.B control node
as yourself, not as root. It uses
.BR sudo (8)
on the control node and
.BR ssh (1)
followed by
.B sudo \-n
on the other nodes. There is no daemon. The same binary must be installed on every node (see
.BR "NODE INSTALLATION" ).
.PP
On every node grantctl creates missing accounts, makes the membership of every role group exact,
writes its own files in
.IR sshd_config.d ,
.I sudoers.d
and
.IR limits.d ,
applies per-user systemd slice limits and disk quotas, and locks or unlocks accounts.
Accounts that are not listed in access.conf are reported and never changed.
.SH HOW A CHANGE IS APPLIED
Every node is changed in the same order:
.IP 1. 4
.B Guards.
A state that would remove the operator's sudo, or passwordless sudo on a node other than the
control node, is refused before anything changes.
.IP 2. 4
.B Widen.
Groups and accounts are created, memberships added, accounts unlocked. Nothing is removed.
.IP 3. 4
.B Switch.
The sudoers, limits and sshd files are backed up and replaced. The new sudoers file is checked with
.B visudo
before it is installed and the whole tree after; the sshd files are checked with
.B sshd \-t
and sshd is reloaded. Any failure restores the backup and stops the run.
.IP 4. 4
.B Narrow.
Only now are memberships removed and accounts locked.
.IP 5. 4
.B Limits.
Slice limits and quotas.
.PP
If a run stops in step 3 the node has its old files and a superset of its old memberships:
nobody who could log in before is locked out.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS Users
.TP
.BI "grant " "user token" "... [\-\-on " nodes "] [\-\-no\-sync]"
Add roles or flags to a user, creating the line if needed, then sync.
.TP
.BI "revoke " user " [" token "...] [\-\-on " nodes "] [\-\-no\-sync]"
Remove roles or flags. With
.B \-\-on
only on those nodes. Without any token the user is dropped from access.conf; with only
.B \-\-on
everything the user holds on those nodes is removed. The account itself is not deleted.
.TP
.BI "lock " user " [\-\-on " nodes "]"
.TQ
.BI "unlock " user " [\-\-on " nodes "]"
Lock an account: the password and SSH keys stop working and running sessions are ended.
.TP
.BI "where " user
Show the account on every node: locked or active, password state, keys, sessions, groups.
.TP
.BI "passwd " user " [\-\-on " nodes "] [\-\-from\-control]"
Set one new temporary password on the user's nodes, with a forced change at next login. With
.B \-\-from\-control
copy the password the user has on the control node to the other nodes instead.
.TP
.BI "delete " user " [\-\-purge] [\-\-yes]"
Delete the account on every node and remove it from access.conf.
.B \-\-purge
also removes the home directory, slice limits and quota record.
.TP
.BI "adopt " user " [\-\-yes]"
Add an existing account to access.conf, drafted from its current state.
.TP
.BR prune " [" \-\-dry\-run ]
Lock accounts that exist on nodes where the user has no access.
.SS Cluster
.TP
.BI "init " "control\-node " [ node "...]"
Write the configuration files and draft access.conf from the current state of the nodes.
Nothing on the nodes changes. Entries that do not map onto a role are marked
.BR review ;
sync refuses to run until they are resolved.
.TP
.BR sync " [" \-\-dry\-run "] [" \fInode\fR ...]
Make nodes match access.conf, control node first.
.B \-\-dry\-run
prints every change, including who gains and who loses SSH access, and changes nothing.
The exit status is 1 when a node was unreachable or failed; the other nodes are still synced.
.TP
.BR audit " [" \fInode\fR ...]
Read-only report of auth-related settings that grantctl does not manage: foreign sshd, sudoers
and limits files, PAM settings, privileged groups, UIDs that differ between nodes.
.TP
.BR status " [" \fInode\fR ...]
Members of the role groups and the owned files on each node.
.TP
.B check
Validate the configuration without contacting any node.
.TP
.BI "plan " node " [\-\-json] [\-\-control\-ip " ip ]
Show what a node should look like. Nothing is contacted.
.TP
.BR list ", " roles ", " version
Print access.conf, the role table, or the version.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-config\-dir " dir"
Configuration directory. Default
.IR /etc/grantctl ;
also settable as
.BR GRANTCTL_CONF .
.TP
.BI \-\-on " nodes"
Comma separated node names. A suffix that matches exactly one node is enough
.RB ( 3
for
.IR node3 ).
.SH CONFIGURATION
.TP
.I /etc/grantctl/access.conf
.nf
<user>  <token> [token ...]   [# comment]
.fi
A token is a role, or a role limited to nodes
.RB ( gpu@gpu1,node3 ),
or a flag:
.B locked
(account locked),
.B nopasswd
(passwordless sudo where the user has sudo),
.B pwonly
(SSH keys refused). Flags can be limited to nodes too.
.TP
.I /etc/grantctl/roles.ini
One section per role.
.RS
.TP 10
.B groups
Unix groups the role puts users in. Required.
.TP
.B nodes
Default nodes:
.B all
or a list. A token
.BI role@ node
overrides it.
.TP
.B password
.B yes
allows SSH password login for the groups.
.TP
.B from
.BR any ,
.B control
(only from the control node's addresses, the jump-host pattern), or a list of source addresses.
.TP
.B sshd
Extra sshd options for the groups:
.IR Key=Value,Key=Value .
.TP
.B sudo
.B yes
writes a sudoers rule for each group.
.TP
.B home
Base directory for accounts created through the role.
.TP
.B slice
systemd slice limits per user, for example
.IR CPUQuota=800%,MemoryMax=20G .
.TP
.B quota
Block quota per user on the filesystem holding
.BR home ,
for example
.IR 90G .
.TP
.B limits
pam_limits for the groups:
.IR item=value,item=value .
.RE
.TP
.I /etc/grantctl/nodes
Node names, one per line, control node first.
.TP
.I /etc/grantctl/grantctl.conf
Optional site settings:
.BR CONTROL ,
.BR SUDO_GROUP ,
.BR SHELL_ROLE ,
and
.BR LEGACY_SSHD ", " LEGACY_SUDO ", " LEGACY_LIMITS ", " LEGACY_SLICE_MARKER ,
which name files of earlier tooling that grantctl replaces on the first sync.
.TP
.I /etc/grantctl/sshd\-baseline
Optional override of the global sshd settings written to every node.
.SH PASSWORDS
The control node is the source. A new account on another node receives the password hash the
user has on the control node, so the password is the same everywhere. Each node remembers the
hash grantctl last set. On a later sync, a password changed on the control node is copied to every
node where the user has not changed it locally; a password changed on a node stays as it is.
Accounts that existed before grantctl are not touched until
.B grantctl passwd
is used for them.
.PP
A user who is new everywhere gets a temporary password on the control node, printed once at the
end of the sync, with a forced change at first login.
.SH NODE INSTALLATION
.TP
.BI "grantctl node install " host ...
Add the PPA
.I ppa:nst\-sdc/grantctl
on each host and install the package.
.TP
.BI "grantctl node install \-\-copy " host ...
Copy the running binary to
.I /usr/local/sbin/grantctl
on each host instead, for hosts without internet access.
.PP
The operator needs SSH access and sudo on the host. After the first sync the operator needs the
.B nopasswd
flag on every node except the control node.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/10\-grantctl.conf
Global sshd settings and AllowGroups.
.TP
.I /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/99\-grantctl.conf
Match blocks: password login, source restrictions, per-group options.
.TP
.I /etc/sudoers.d/99\-grantctl
The sudo group rule, role rules and NOPASSWD lines.
.TP
.I /etc/security/limits.d/60\-grantctl.conf
pam_limits per group.
.TP
.I /etc/systemd/system/user\-UID.slice.d/50\-grantctl.conf
Slice limits per user.
.TP
.I /var/backups/grantctl/
Backups of the three configuration directories, one per change.
.TP
.I /var/lib/grantctl/
Recorded quotas and password hashes set by grantctl (root only).
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success. 1 when the configuration is invalid, a node was unreachable, or a node refused or
failed a change.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
grantctl init node1 node2 node3 gpu1     # draft the roster from the nodes
grantctl check && grantctl sync \-\-dry\-run
grantctl grant maya student              # new user, default nodes of the role
grantctl grant maya gpu \-\-on gpu1
grantctl where maya
grantctl revoke maya gpu
grantctl lock maya
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sshd_config (5),
.BR sudoers (5),
.BR limits.conf (5),
.BR systemd.resource\-control (5),
.BR useradd (8)
